1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,850
Monday, 9 AM.

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:03,650
The payment page is down.

3
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:05,950
Customers cannot pay.

4
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:09,150
Chats, emails and calls are pouring in…

5
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:12,850
…but nobody knows who owns the problem.

6
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:14,950
We built the solution.

7
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:19,750
This is I-ITSM: Intelligent IT Service Management.

8
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:14,350
We are Group 9.

9
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:18,050
I-ITSM takes an incident from start to close:

10
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:21,150
classify, prioritise, assign…

11
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:23,950
…track the SLA, resolve and verify.

12
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:30,450
Our innovation is an AI triage copilot with voice reporting, powered by Gemini,

13
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:34,750
with a rule-based fallback if the AI is down.

14
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:41,750
React for the front end, Spring Boot for the back end, PostgreSQL for the database, all in Docker.

15
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:47,150
There are three roles: administrator, employee and technician.

16
00:01:47,550 --> 00:01:51,350
I'm Likhil, the administrator. Let's configure the system.

17
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:53,250
I'm currently signing in as the admin.

18
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,400
The assignment strategy is switchable at runtime,

19
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:00,900
with no downtime.

20
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:07,200
There are three strategies: skill-based, load-based and deadline-aware assignment.

21
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:11,467
SLA policies are fully configurable.

22
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:13,467
For the purposes of this demo,

23
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:15,967
I'll set the response time to 1 minute

24
00:02:16,467 --> 00:02:19,067
and the resolution time to 5 minutes.

25
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:22,450
I try creating a new account.

26
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:29,400
The system says my account is pending administrator approval.

27
00:02:31,834 --> 00:02:34,134
A new self-registered user is waiting.

28
00:02:34,534 --> 00:02:37,434
No one gets in without admin approval.

29
00:02:37,734 --> 00:02:38,334
Approved.

30
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:42,250
I'll try a wrong email and password

31
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:43,150
5 times.

32
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:48,667
This is a security feature:

33
00:02:48,867 --> 00:02:51,967
it blocks repeated failed logins from the same IP.

34
00:02:53,017 --> 00:02:54,417
Signing in as an employee.

35
00:02:57,067 --> 00:02:59,367
Here I can just click 'Speak the problem'

36
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:01,767
and it will fill in

37
00:03:02,167 --> 00:03:03,467
the description for me.

38
00:03:05,534 --> 00:03:07,134
🎙 "The payment page shows…

39
00:03:07,334 --> 00:03:09,634
…an error 500 at checkout.

40
00:03:09,834 --> 00:03:12,534
Customers cannot pay. Everyone is affected.

41
00:03:12,734 --> 00:03:13,734
This is urgent."

42
00:03:15,134 --> 00:03:17,334
It takes a few moments to transcribe

43
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:18,470
the description.

44
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:26,370
It auto-fills the category, affected service, impact, urgency and number of affected users.

45
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:29,270
It also gives suggestions

46
00:03:36,184 --> 00:03:36,784
based on the description.

47
00:03:32,337 --> 00:03:34,037
I can still edit the description.

48
00:03:36,134 --> 00:03:39,034
This smart feature can accept any language.

49
00:03:39,234 --> 00:03:42,634
For example, I'll try a Hindi problem statement right now.

50
00:03:44,251 --> 00:03:46,651
🎙  (speaking in Hindi)

51
00:03:48,617 --> 00:03:50,617
It transcribes the statement for me.

52
00:03:51,502 --> 00:03:53,602
Now let's try creating a duplicate ticket.

53
00:03:55,152 --> 00:03:59,652
This is what another employee affected by the same problem would likely do.

54
00:04:00,352 --> 00:04:01,852
We don't want to overload the technician,

55
00:04:02,452 --> 00:04:05,852
so the system automatically checks for duplicates.

56
00:04:09,285 --> 00:04:11,585
It says: 'Duplicate of #58'

57
00:04:13,485 --> 00:04:15,085
because the descriptions match

58
00:04:15,385 --> 00:04:16,985
and the priorities are the same.

59
00:04:17,685 --> 00:04:20,285
The system flagged this as a duplicate.

60
00:04:21,119 --> 00:04:23,119
Now I'll create a second ticket.

61
00:04:26,369 --> 00:04:29,369
Let's try the AI suggestion.

62
00:04:36,952 --> 00:04:39,552
It set the impact to high and the urgency to high

63
00:04:42,236 --> 00:04:43,936
and set the priority to P1.

64
00:04:31,369 --> 00:04:33,949
It automatically filled this in, and

65
00:04:34,152 --> 00:04:36,052
also calculated the priority.

66
00:04:44,569 --> 00:04:48,169
…and I instantly got a notification for the new incident.

67
00:04:48,669 --> 00:04:51,869
I'll now log in with Alice's credentials.

68
00:04:54,786 --> 00:04:56,286
This is my workload,

69
00:04:56,886 --> 00:05:00,686
the workload strip and the incidents assigned to me.

70
00:05:01,686 --> 00:05:07,586
As you can see, the P1 incidents #58 and #59 have been assigned to me.

71
00:05:08,553 --> 00:05:13,353
We can't resolve the incident directly. We first need to acknowledge it and follow the steps.

72
00:05:14,936 --> 00:05:15,836
So I'll acknowledge it,

73
00:05:17,036 --> 00:05:20,436
and then, in the notes, I'll type the first findings.

74
00:05:24,536 --> 00:05:25,636
I'll click…

75
00:05:26,836 --> 00:05:28,636
…Request Info,

76
00:05:29,236 --> 00:05:29,936
and I'll type.

77
00:05:33,036 --> 00:05:34,536
Now I need more information,

78
00:05:35,336 --> 00:05:38,036
so I'll ask the employee directly.

79
00:05:37,953 --> 00:05:40,353
The technician requested additional information.

80
00:05:42,903 --> 00:05:43,903
I answer his question.

81
00:05:47,186 --> 00:05:49,086
So now I'll refresh my screen,

82
00:05:50,786 --> 00:05:53,086
and here we can see the employee reply.

83
00:05:53,486 --> 00:05:55,286
Now I'll click Resolve

84
00:05:56,286 --> 00:05:57,386
and type the resolution details.

85
00:06:01,469 --> 00:06:02,869
Now we click OK.

86
00:06:04,903 --> 00:06:06,203
Now we'll check the history.

87
00:06:07,103 --> 00:06:09,803
Here we can see that the incident was created,

88
00:06:10,403 --> 00:06:12,403
and the employee has replied.

89
00:06:12,903 --> 00:06:16,903
I resolved it with the details, which is mandatory,

90
00:06:17,103 --> 00:06:18,503
and the audit trail records who did what, and when.

91
00:06:18,719 --> 00:06:19,619
I'm signing in.

92
00:06:23,002 --> 00:06:29,402
I'm Bob. This ticket was assigned to me by skill, but I was busy. Nobody acknowledged it in time.

93
00:06:29,702 --> 00:06:30,802
The system warned me,

94
00:06:31,702 --> 00:06:34,452
recorded an SLA violation, then automatically

95
00:06:34,542 --> 00:06:35,442
escalated.

96
00:06:36,285 --> 00:06:37,285
Now I resolve it.

97
00:06:41,202 --> 00:06:48,869
Now I take it, fix it and resolve it. Resolving lowers my workload count,

98
00:06:45,465 --> 00:06:48,065
so the next assignment stays fair.

99
00:06:48,769 --> 00:06:51,569
Here I can verify and rate resolved incidents.

100
00:06:53,419 --> 00:06:54,919
The feedback is optional.

101
00:06:58,519 --> 00:06:59,819
The rating helps rank

102
00:06:59,936 --> 00:07:01,036
suggested resolutions.

103
00:07:01,536 --> 00:07:03,936
Now we will look at the source code.

104
00:07:04,736 --> 00:07:07,636
This is an interface called AssignmentStrategy,

105
00:07:09,353 --> 00:07:11,653
and this is a class implementing that interface.

106
00:07:12,269 --> 00:07:17,869
This is the Strategy pattern: three assignment algorithms, one interface, switchable at runtime.

107
00:07:19,269 --> 00:07:21,869
Now this is the IncidentEventPublisher class.

108
00:07:23,769 --> 00:07:26,969
Observer pattern: one event, four observers:

109
00:07:27,969 --> 00:07:38,469
audit, in-app notification, email and webhook. Adding a new channel means adding a class. This is the AuditObserver, implementing the IncidentObserver interface.

110
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:43,420
This is the IncidentFactory class: the Factory pattern for creating incidents.

111
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,520
And this is the IncidentClassifier interface.

112
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:50,720
This is the GeminiClassifier,

113
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:55,720
Gemini sits behind our own interface, and if it fails, the keyword classifier takes over.

114
00:07:56,670 --> 00:07:59,570
Now this is the incident lifecycle manager,

115
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:04,420
a state-style lifecycle manager where invalid transitions are rejected.

116
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:14,520
Here is the abstract class ApiException, extending RuntimeException. Here we have implemented a very important OOP concept: inheritance.

117
00:08:15,087 --> 00:08:18,987
This is the User.java file, where we implemented encapsulation,

118
00:08:19,387 --> 00:08:22,787
since all the instance variables are declared private.

119
00:08:24,687 --> 00:08:27,187
So the password is never sent back;

120
00:08:27,387 --> 00:08:29,287
it is write-only.

121
00:08:29,587 --> 00:08:31,787
Now I am opening TEST_CASES.md.

122
00:08:33,487 --> 00:08:36,387
We wrote automated tests for the lifecycle,

123
00:08:37,187 --> 00:08:41,187
login lockout, assignment strategy, validation, priorities,

124
00:08:42,487 --> 00:08:47,187
duplicates, SLA monitoring, AI fallback and authorization. They all pass.

125
00:08:47,937 --> 00:08:50,237
Now I am opening report.md.

126
00:08:51,837 --> 00:08:57,037
Security: BCrypt, JWT, role-based access checked on every request,

127
00:08:57,237 --> 00:08:59,637
login lockout, server-side validation,

128
00:09:00,437 --> 00:09:08,737
and a safe AI layer. The AI answer is checked against our own lists, so prompt injection cannot invent categories.

129
00:09:09,237 --> 00:09:10,637
This is Mission Control.

130
00:09:11,837 --> 00:09:14,837
Here we have the activity feed and the heatmap.

131
00:09:15,137 --> 00:09:20,337
The rings show the tickets closest to their SLA deadlines.

132
00:09:21,337 --> 00:09:25,737
Updates from the other three roles show up in real time.

133
00:09:27,587 --> 00:09:29,587
Here are the SLA violations.

134
00:09:30,287 --> 00:09:33,487
The system warns the technician at 80%,

135
00:09:33,887 --> 00:09:36,687
and it also records and saves the violations.

136
00:09:38,487 --> 00:09:40,387
Here are the reports and dashboards.

137
00:09:40,687 --> 00:09:42,187
Here we have the totals,

138
00:09:42,587 --> 00:09:45,387
the incident overview and technician workload.

139
00:09:45,887 --> 00:09:49,287
We can also see the number of incidents per service.

140
00:09:51,087 --> 00:09:52,787
Here is ticket governance.

141
00:09:53,087 --> 00:09:55,287
The admin can search tickets,

142
00:09:55,687 --> 00:09:56,387
filter them,

143
00:09:56,787 --> 00:10:00,087
and check the ticket history.

144
00:10:00,687 --> 00:10:01,287
Also,

145
00:10:01,587 --> 00:10:02,987
if a ticket is resolved,

146
00:10:03,287 --> 00:10:04,387
we can close it.

147
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:07,920
Here is the mailbox.

148
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:11,520
Notifications are also sent through email.

149
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:13,420
For this project,

150
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:16,420
we simulate the emails.

151
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:18,420
All the emails

152
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:21,620
sent to all accounts can be seen over here,

153
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:26,920
and the emails and notifications specific to the admin are seen over here.
